late. `parse_date(\"1985-001\")` returns `1985-01-02` and `parse_date(\"1985-102\")` returns April 13 instead of April 12. The cause is in `isodate/isodates.py` at the line `return ret + timedelta(days=int(groups[\"day\"]))`: it adds the day number to January 1st, so it should add `day - 1`. Formatting is correct (it writes `1985-102` for April 12), so parsing and formatting disagree. That's why the round-trip tests fail too. Every failing test involves an ordinal date:\n- ordinal dates in basic, extended and 6-digit-year forms\n- a check of every day of 1900, 1985, 2000 and 2024\n- format-then-parse round trips for ordinal dates and date-times\n- parsing date-times with an ordinal date part\n\n**What the suite covers:**\n- **Dates:** every supported format, including week dates where week 1 starts in the previous year, 6-digit years with a sign, and default month/day.\n- **Times:** fractional hours, minutes and seconds, with extra digits cut off rather than rounded; time zones `Z`, `±hh:mm`, `±hhmm` and `±hh`.\n- **Date-times:** parsing, formatting and invalid input.\n- **Durations:** parsing (returns a `timedelta` or a `Duration`), negative, fractional and alternative forms, formatting, and round trips.\n- **The `Duration` class:** adding to and subtracting from dates, including month-end clamping (Jan 31 + 1 month = Feb 29 in a leap year) and leap years; equality, hashing, pickling, multiplication and `totimedelta`.\n- **Helpers and time zones:** the month-arithmetic helper `fquotmod`, `max_days_in_month`, the UTC and fixed-offset time zone classes, and the public exports.\n\n**Cases I dropped:** I removed four of my own assertions because the library deliberately allows that input, so they weren't bugs. It accepts month `00` (falls back to the default month) and empty-time durations like `PT` and `P1DT`. The fourth used Python's built-in `timezone` with a `time` object, which Python itself doesn't support.\n\nTo get a fully passing run, fix that one line in `iso